24 - 828\nL129: 5\" x 7.4\" (12.7 x 18.8 cm)  | 24 - 828  | 24 - 776  | 24 - 812  | 72 - 600  | 24 - 828\nL130: 5.04\" x 7.17\" (12.8 x 18.2 cm)  | 24 - 828  | 24 - 776  | 24 - 812  | 72 - 600  | 24 - 828\nL131: 5.83\" x 8.27\" (14.8 x 21.0 cm)  | 24 - 828  | 24 - 776  | 24 - 812  | 72 - 600  | 24 - 828\nL132: 5.98\" x 8.58\" (15.2 x 21.8 cm)  | 24 - 828  | 24 - 776  | 24 - 812  | 72 - 600  | 24 - 828\nL133: 5.98\" x 8.94\"  (15.2 x 22.7 cm)  | 24 - 828  | 24 - 776  | 24 - 812  | 72 - 600  | 24 - 828\nL134: Large Trim Sizes\nL135: 7.17\" x 10.12\" (18.2 x 25.7 cm)  | 24 - 828  | 24 - 776  | 24 - 812  | 72 - 600  | 24 - 828\nL136: 7.17\" x 8.11\" (18.2 x 20.6 cm)  | 24 - 828  | 24 - 776  | 24 - 812  | 72 - 600  | 24 - 828\nL137: 8.27\" x 10.12\" (21.0 x 25.7 cm)  | 24 - 780  | 24 - 730  | 24 - 764  | 72 - 600  | 24 - 780\nL138: 8.27\" x 11.69\" (21.0 x 29.7 cm)  | 24 - 780  | 24 - 730  | 24 - 764  | Not available  | 24 - 780\nL139: Note:\nL140:   * Hardcover is not available in kdp.amazon.co.jp\nL141:   * Standard color is currently unavailable for Amazon.co.jp\nL142: \nL143: cite47†^ Back to top L144: \nL145: * * *\nL146: ### Bleed\nL147: \nL148: cite48†iframe†www.youtube.com L149: Bleed is a printing term that refers to when printed items on a page, such as images or illustrations, reach all the way to the edge of the page. All book covers on KDP require bleed. However, you can choose whether your interior has bleed or not.\nL150: Note: Bleed is only supported for fixed-format files like print-ready PDFs.\nL151: \nL152: * * *\nL153: Book interior during file setup:\nL154: No bleed (left) and with bleed (right)  | Book interior after final trim:\nL155: No bleed (left) and with bleed (right)\nL156: cite49†Image: Book interior during file setup showing no bleed on left and with bleed on right†m.media-amazon.com | cite50†Image: Book interior after final trim showing no bleed on left and with bleed on right†m.media-amazon.com L157: \nL158: * * *\nL159: When a book is printed, all pages are trimmed to the selected trim size by cutting 0.125\" (3.2 mm) from the top, bottom, and outside edges. In order for objects on your page to reach all the way to the edge of the page, those objects must extend past where the page will be trimmed by 0.125\" (3.2 mm). This means you need to increase your page size beyond where it will be trimmed.\nL160: \nL161: Examples of page size with and without bleed(kdp.amazon.com)\nL162: Page size without bleed  | Page size with bleed\nL163: 5\" x 8\" (12.7 x 20.32 cm)  | 5.125\" x 8.25\" (13.01 x 20.96 cm)\nL164: 5.06\" x 7.81\" (12.85 x19.84 cm)  | 5.185\" x 8.06\" (13.17 x 20.47 cm)\nL165: 5.25\" x 8\" (13.34 x 20.32 cm)  | 5.375\" x 8.25\" (13.65 x 20.96 cm)\nL166: 5.5\" x 8.5\" (13.97 x 21.59 cm)  | 5.626\" x 8.75\" (14.29 x 22.23 cm)\nL167: 6\" x 9\" (15.24 x 22.86 cm)  | 6.125\" x 9.25\" (15.56 x 23.50 cm)\nL168: Large Trim Sizes\nL169: 6.14\" x 9.21\" (15.6 x 23.39 cm)  | 6.265\" x 9.46\" (15.9 x 23.99 cm)\nL170: 6.69\" x 9.61\" (16.99 x 24.4 cm)  | 6.815\" x 9.86\" (17.29 x 25 cm)\nL171: 7\" x 10\" (17.78 x 25.4 cm)  | 7.125\" x 10.25\" (18.08 x 26 cm)\nL172: 7.44\" x 9.69\" (18.9 x 24.61 cm)  | 7.565\" x 9.94\" (19.2 x 25.21 cm)\nL173: 7.5\" x 9.25\" (19.05 x 23.5 cm)  | 7.625\" x 9.5\" (19.35 x 24.1 cm)\nL174: 8\" x 10\" (20.32 x 25.4 cm)  | 8.125\" x 10.25\" (20.52 x 26 cm)\nL175: 8.5\" x 11\" (21.59 x 27.94 cm)  | 8.625\" x 11.25\" (21.89 x 28.54 cm)\nL176: 8.25\" x 6\" (20.96 x 15.24 cm)  | 8.375\" x 6.25\" (21.26 x 15.84 cm)\nL177: 8.25\" x 8.25\" (20.96 x 20.96 cm)  | 8.375\" x 8.5\" (21.26 x 21.56 cm)\nL178: 8.25\" x 11\" (20.96 x 27.94 cm)  | 8.375\" x 11.25\" (21.26 x 28.54 cm)\nL179: 8.27\" x 11.69\" (21 x 29.7 cm)  | 8.395\" x 11.94\" (21.3 x 30.3 cm)\nL180: 8.5\" x 8.5\" (21.59 x 21.59 cm)  | 8.625\" x 8.75\" (21.91 x 22.23 cm)\nL181: Examples of page size with and without bleed (kdp.amazon.co.jp)\nL182: Page size without bleed  | Page size with bleed\nL183: 4.06\" x 7.17\" (10.3 x 18.2 cm)  | 4.18\" x 7.42\" (10.62 x 18.84 cm)\nL184: 4.13\" x 6.81\" (10.5 x 17.3 cm)  | 4.26\" x 7.06\" (10.82 x 17.94 cm)\nL185: 4.41\" x 6.85\" (11.2 x 17.4 cm)  | 4.53\" x 7.10\" (11.52 x 18.04 cm)\nL186: 5\" x 7.4\" (12.7 x 18.8 cm)  | 5.13\" x 7.65\" (13.02 x 19.44 cm)\nL187: 5.04\" x 7.17\" (12.8 x 18.2 cm)  | 5.16\" x 7.42\" (13.12 x 18.84 cm)\nL188: 5.83\" x 8.27\" (14.8 x 21.0 cm)  | 5.95\" x 8.52\" (15.12 x 21.64 cm)\nL189: 5.98\" x 8.58\" (15.2 x 21.8 cm)  | 6.11\" x 8.83\" (15.52 x 22.44 cm)\nL190: 5.98\" x 8.94\" (15.2 x 22.7 cm)  | 6.11\" x 9.19\" (15.52 x 23.34 cm)\nL191: Large Trim Sizes\nL192: 7.17\" x 10.12\" (18.2 x 25.7 cm)  | 7.29\" x 10.37\" (18.52 x 26.34 cm)\nL193: 7.17\" x 8.11\" (18.2 x 20.6 cm)  | 7.29\" x 8.36\" (18.52 x 21.24 cm)\nL194: 8.27\" x 10.12\" (21.0 x 25.7 cm)  | 8.39\" x 10.37\" (21.32 x 26.34 cm)\nL195: 8.27\" x 11.69\" (21.0 x 29.7 cm)  | 8.39\" x 11.94\" (21.32 x 30.33 cm)\nL196: * * *\nL197: #### When to choose bleed for your interior\nL198: You should include bleed in your interior file if you have any images, backgrounds, or illustrations in your book that you want to reach the edge of the page.\nL199: Note: Even if just one page in your interior requires bleed, then your entire file should be set up with bleed.\nL200: Your paperback manuscript file must be sized correctly for bleed to prevent a white border from appearing at the edge of the page when the book is trimmed. To prevent the white border, increase your page size and ensure that the image extends beyond the trim line. This way, when the book is trimmed to size, the image will reach all the way to the edge. For help calculating your book dimensions, cite51†try our calculator .\nL201: Alternatively, use the following formula to calculate your page width and height with bleed:\nL202: Inches  |  | Centimeters\nL203: (Trim height) + (0.125\" x 2 (bleed)) = Page height with bleed\nL204: (Trim width) +(0.125\" (bleed)) = Page width with bleed  |  | (Trim Height) + (3.2 mm x 2 (bleed)) = Page height with bleed\nL205: (Trim Width) + (3.2 mm (bleed)) = Page width with bleed\nL206: For example, if your trim size is 6 x 9 inches (15.24 x 22.86 cm), set the page size to 6.125 x 9.25 inches (15.54 x 23.46 cm).\nL207: Bleed manuscript layout for a 6 x 9 inch (15.24 x 22.86 cm) book:\nL208: cite52†Image: Bleed manuscript layout diagram for a 6 x 9 inch book†m.media-amazon.com L209: cite47†^ Back to top L210: * * *\nL211: ### Margins\nL212: \nL213: cite53†iframe†www.youtube.com L214: Margins protect your content from running off the page and being cut off during the manufacturing process. The top, bottom, and outside margins must be a minimum of 0.25 inches  for books without bleed and 0.375 inches (9.6mm) for books with bleed. The inside margin size depends on the page count because the higher the number of pages, the thicker the book will become, which means the inside margin must also increase to allow enough space for the spine.\nL215: Note: The inside margin is sometimes called the \"gutter.\" Some software programs will have separate fields for both the inside margin and the gutter. Make sure to input the same value for your inside margin and the gutter.\nL216: Minimum margin size by page count (with and without bleed):\nL217: Page count  | Inside (gutter) margins  | Outside margins (no bleed)  | Outside margins (with bleed)\nL218: 24 to 150 pages  | 0.375\" in (9.6 mm)  | at least 0.25 in (6.4 mm)  | at least 0.375 in (9.6 mm)\nL219: 151 to 300 pages  | 0.5 in (12.7 mm)  | at least 0.25 in (6.4 mm)  | at least 0.375 in (9.6 mm)\nL220: 301 to 500 pages  | 0.625 in (15.9 mm)  | at least 0.25 in (6.4 mm)  | at least 0.375 in (9.6 mm)\nL221: 501 to 700 pages  | 0.75 in (19.1 mm)  | at least 0.25 in (6.4 mm)  | at least 0.375 in (9.6 mm)\nL222: 701 to 828 pages  | 0.875 in (22.3 mm)  | at least 0.25 in (6.4 mm)  | at least 0.375 in (9.6 mm)\nL223: Note: your top, bottom, and outside margins do not have to be the same. For example, you can set the top and bottom margin to 1 inch, and the outside margin to 0.375 inches as long as they all meet the required minimum.\nL224: cite47†^ Back to top L225: * * *\nL226: ### Formatting in Microsoft Word or Pages for Mac\nL227: \nL228: See our step-by-step guides for formatting using Microsoft Word, or Pages for Mac.\nL229: Note: These guides may not be up to date for your current software version.\nL230: \nL231:   * cite54†Build Your Book - Word for Windows L232:   * cite55†Build Your Book - Word for Mac L233:   * cite56†Build Your Book - Pages for Mac L234: cite47†^ Back to top L235: \nL236: * * *\nL237: #### Was this article helpful? cite57†Yes†pulse.amazon | cite57†No†pulse.amazon L238: \nL239: Make more money and reach more readers through Kindle Unlimited. cite58†Learn More L240: \nL241: cite59†Image: KDP Select logo†images-na.ssl-images-amazon.com L242: \nL243: Total KDP Select Author Earnings\nL244: \nL245: August 2026\nL246: \nL247:  •\nL248: \nL249: $69.4 Million\nL250: \nL251: © 1996-2026, Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates. All Rights Reserved.\nL252: Amazon and Kindle are trademarks of Amazon.com Inc. or its affiliates.\nL253: \nL254: cite60†KDP Select Earn more money andreach new readers --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n9 Tips for Interior Book Design (https://www.ingramspark.com/blog/9-tips-for-interior-book-design)\nciteturn14view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn13search6\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 70\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: This is a search field with an auto-suggest feature attached. [Input: Search] [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4:   * There are no suggestions because the search field is empty.\nL5: \nL6: \nL7: \nL8: cite1†Back to Blog L9: \nL10: cite2†Image L11: # 9 Tips for Interior Book Design\nL12: \nL13: Tuesday, November 07, 2017\nL14: \nL15: by cite3†IngramSpark Staff (cite4†@ingramspark†twitter.com )\nL16: Your interior book design is critical when it comes to making your book presentable and, even more important, readable. Some of the most common mistakes authors and publishers make when it comes to interior book design include omitting hyphens, incorrect margin size, imperfect justification, and allowing widow/orphan lines of text.\nL17: Designing a book's interior might not seem like as much fun as choosing a cite5†book cover design , but it can be if you approach it in steps and follow a few practical cite6†guidelines .\nL18: ### 1. Determine the Size of Your Book First\nL19: Consider the length and genre of the book before choosing an under- or over-sized cite7†trim size . A particularly long nonfiction work about the Napoleonic Wars might be too chunky to be a comfortable read if it's printed in a 6\" x 9\" format. Similarly, a short but lively romance novel might be best read in a smaller format.\nL20: You must first decide on the trim size of the book so you can see how your chapter headings, font, pagination, and everything else fits in with the book's overall design.cite8†Image: person researching L21: ### 2. Don't Get Too Cute with Your Font\nL22: \nL23: Sometimes it's best to go with the tried-and-true and choosing your font is one of those times. Unless you're working on an illustrated or artsy book, such as one of photographs, study the fonts in books you've read and enjoyed yourself. There is a reason why Times New Roman, Garamond, and Bookman fonts are so popular.\nL24: ### 3. Use a Typical Font Size\nL25: \nL26: Again, unless you're working on a special edition or writing highly experimental fiction, use a standard 11- to 12-point font size. It might be tempting to use a large typeface, especially if you have trouble with small print yourself. However, you should reserve large fonts for \"large print\" books printed specifically for those with vision problems.\nL27: ### 4. Get Creative with Chapter Pages\nL28: \nL29: The start of a new chapter is one place where you can play with font. It is perfectly acceptable to choose a unique typeface for the number and/or title of a new chapter. In fact, using a drop cap for the first letter of chapter text provides a visual \"new start\" and can improve readability. If you choose to write chapter titles, printing them in larger, bold, or unique fonts sets them apart and adds to the reading experience.\nL30: ### 5. Mind Your Headers and Footers\nL31: \nL32: Be careful not to put too much text in your headers and footers. You must include page numbers, so consider the length of the book title and chapter titles before deciding if and where to place them. Refer to books you have read in the past to see what works well and what you like.\nL33: ### 6. Mind Your Margins\nL34: \nL35: Make the bound, or gutter, side of the margins wider than the margins on the outer side of the page. This leaves enough room for the book to be bound properly and makes it easier for the reader to bend pages and read all the text comfortably. It can be difficult to read at a steady pace if a book's inside margins are too close to the binding.\nL36: ### 7. Pay Attention to \"Special\" Pages\nL37: cite9†Image: designing a book These include the pages containing dedications, acknowledgements, indices, copyright, foreword, afterword, and others. The cite10†copyright page is extremely important as it will contain legal protections that reserve author rights and provide a shield against potential lawsuits. Make sure you place special pages in the proper place as well. Forewords, introductions, and dedications belong in the front of your book.\nL38: The afterword, acknowledgements, book club discussion questions, and bibliographies belong in the back.\nL39: ### 8. Be Consistent\nL40: Whatever you select for each element of your interior text design should remain consistent throughout the book. If you start with a 12-point typeface, with the exception of chapter pages or perhaps the dedication page, stay with a 12-point typeface through the end of the book. And whether you begin writing your masterpiece adhering to the Chicago Manual of Style or the Associated Press Stylebook, be consistent with that as well.\nL41: Lack of consistency is one of the hallmarks of a poorly written and/or shoddily produced book.\nL42: ### 9. Ask for Help\nL43: \nL44: If you're completely flummoxed or too overwhelmed to know where to begin, don't be shy about seeking help from a guidebook or cite11†designer . You've done the hard work of writing the book; maybe interior text design is just not your thing and that's okay.\nL45: Be creative and brave, albeit not too brave, with the interior text design of your soon-to-be-published book. For expanded guidance on typeface, lead space, pagination, copyrights, front and back matter, and much more, download the free cite12†Pocket Guide to Publishing today!\nL46: \nL47: \nL48: \nL49: cite13†Image: IngramSpark Staff L50: \nL51: IngramSpark® is an award-winning independent publishing platform, offering indie authors and publishers the ability to create, manage, and globally distribute print and ebooks.\nL52: ## Trending\nL53: \nL54: \nL55: \nL56: ###\nL57: \nL58: ### Recommended Articles\nL59: \nL60: ### JOIN OUR MAILING LIST\nL61: \nL62: By clicking the \"submit\" button, you are agreeing to receive future marketing e-mail messages from IngramSpark.\nL63: ### Quick Links\nL64: \nL65: © Copyright Lightning Source LLC. All Rights Reserved.\nL66: \nL67: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com cite14†Image†www.facebook.com L68: \nL69: .legal-consent-container, .legal-consent-container * { color: #ffffff !important; } .legal-consent-container a, .legal-consent-container a:visited, .legal-consent-container a:hover { color: #ffffff !important; }\n"
